## Why This Is Asked

Shows ability to design a pluggable, runtime-extensible validation system that minimizes gateway churn while supporting new rules added at runtime. Emphasizes clean interfaces and parallel execution semantics.

## Key Concepts

- Registry pattern for runtime extensibility
- Strategy-like Validator interface
- Parallel validation with Promise.all
- Fail-fast per field to return actionable errors
- Lightweight, testable integration points

## Code Example

```typescript
type ValidationResult = { ok: boolean; error?: string };

interface Validator {
  name: string;
  validate(value: any): Promise<ValidationResult>;
}

class ValidatorRegistry {
  private registry = new Map<string, (opts?: any) => Validator>();
  register(name: string, factory: (opts?: any) => Validator) {
    this.registry.set(name, factory);
  }
  get(name: string, opts?: any): Validator | null {
    const f = this.registry.get(name);
    return f ? f(opts) : null;
  }
}
```

```typescript
// Usage example (sketch)
const reg = new ValidatorRegistry();
reg.register('notEmpty', () => ({ name: 'notEmpty', validate: async (v)=> ({ ok: v !== null && v !== '' }), }));
reg.register('isEmail', () => ({ name: 'isEmail', validate: async (v)=> ({ ok: /^[^@\s]+@[^\s]+\.[^\s]+$/.test(v), error: 'invalid email' }) }));

const routeValidators = [{name:'notEmpty'},{name:'isEmail'}];
const validators = routeValidators.map(v => reg.get(v.name)).filter(v => v) as Validator[];
// per-field validation example
const results = await Promise.all(validators.map(v => v.validate(input[fieldName])));
```

